## Findings and exact boundaries

The executed cases cover exact UTF-8 output, exact stream caps, separate stdout/stderr overflow, active timeout, actual owned grandchild cleanup, nonzero exit, failed spawn, caller argument/environment/source-list mutation, malformed limits, stable and changed/deleted sources, exclusive diagnostic writes, and a source larger than the per-file allowance. Every recorded live child/descendant PID returned `ESRCH` in the independent follow-up liveness check. The grandchild case recorded parent 46440 and child 32796; both were absent. Each overflow/deadline fixture returned a closed failed run and a successful owned-PID `taskkill /T /F` result. The spawned synthetic processes were the only process targets; this review never targeted a process name or unrelated user process.

I found no new reproduced failure within this supported contract. A second read-only static reviewer independently examined the exact helper and reported the already-disclosed exited-parent descendant boundary described below. That reviewer executed no code and changed no files. The additional review does not expand the tested Windows scenarios.

The helper starts the current Node executable with explicit captured string arguments, an absolute working directory, a copied environment, `shell:false` and hidden Windows windows (helper lines 27–32). Argument count, individual length and aggregate character limits are enforced before spawning. `NODE_TEST_CONTEXT` is removed only from the child environment. The caller-mutation regressions establish isolation for string arguments, primitive environment entries and the source-list strings actually used by the trusted collector; they do not establish isolation for arbitrary accessor-bearing objects or mutable objects used as environment values. Other trusted inherited Node/environment settings are retained. This helper is not an untrusted-input sandbox.

Default child timeout is 30 seconds, retained stdout is 4 MiB and stderr is 1 MiB, and cleanup-helper timeout is 5 seconds (line 7). Allowed maxima are 120 seconds, 8 MiB per stream and 10-second cleanup (line 31). Exact cap-sized output succeeds. Bytes beyond either retained cap set a failure, pause both streams and initiate cleanup; retained raw buffers are clipped to their respective caps (lines 35–43). These bounds cover retained output, not OS pipe buffers, child memory, aggregate process memory or event-loop preemption. Cleanup command diagnostics share a separate 16 KiB retention limit (line 22). The close guard is `2 × cleanupMs + 1000` milliseconds after stopping, or 11 seconds by default (line 35). Its escape path leaves `closed:false`, preventing qualification as success. These failed-helper/close-guard fallback branches were read statically, not forced in the 17 tests.

Windows cleanup uses the PID obtained from the owned child object and the system `taskkill.exe` with `/T /F` (lines 17–24). It never enumerates all Node processes. A zero taskkill exit code is a cleanup-command result, not proof that every descendant can never survive. There is no atomic Windows Job Object boundary or retained descendant inventory, and PID reuse or descendants changing their parent/escaping the tree remain possible. In particular, if the root has already exited, line 13 returns `confirmed:true` together with `descendantGuarantee:false` and does not run taskkill. A descendant that keeps inherited pipes open can survive the eventual timeout, while a descendant with independent pipes can survive a normal successful root exit. This exact source path is a static finding, not an additional executed reproduction. It is already explicitly disclosed by the preserved stage documentation and current integration receipt. Failed taskkill can fall back only to signaling the root (line 35); that path explicitly withholds a descendant guarantee. The POSIX process-group branch was not executed in this Windows review.

Source names/paths and caller settings are copied before the first fingerprint await (lines 53–58). Fingerprinting streams accept at most 8 MiB per source and 64 MiB aggregate across at most 128 identities (lines 46–57). Stream I/O can read a chunk past the threshold before rejecting, without retaining it as evidence. Those byte limits do not provide a filesystem wall-clock deadline. Fingerprinting occurs before and after the child; matching hashes show endpoint equality, not continuous immutability or protection against a transient change restored between checks. Mutation/deletion fixtures correctly reject success and retain diagnostics. Three diagnostic files are sequential exclusive writes (`wx`, line 62); they preserve existing file bytes but do not form an atomic multi-file transaction. An I/O failure can leave a partial diagnostic set and rejects the call. The tests cover an existing stdout file, not every I/O-failure ordering.

The geometry collector was examined statically only. Bootstrap fetch/local archive reads, synchronous decompression, study inventories, application-source reads and additional in-process legacy oracle work precede or follow the bounded child operation (collector lines 18–20). Official fetch accumulation has a 40 MiB cap and decompression has a 256 MiB output cap, but fetch and filesystem operations have no hard end-to-end deadline; local archive reads and study inventory `readFile` calls also allocate before independent size/hash acceptance. Source fingerprints and before/after collector/helper identities still gate the child-backed publication path. Published geometry records are separate sequential writes after validation, rather than an atomic publication transaction. This review did not rerun or rebind the historical geometry receipt to the current collector.

The independent wrapper itself has a 60-second child deadline and 1 MiB per output stream; neither limit was hit. Its failure path is a root-only signal, so this successful run receipt does not assert a general tree-cleanup guarantee for the reviewer wrapper. Its separately owned retained observation directory was validated as a direct `os.tmpdir()` child with the expected prefix, copied here, and removed. Production tests remove their own separately created temporary directory. No real user data, external accounts, browser state, performance measurement or full-product claim is included.

The final `review-validation.json` binds this report, the private harness, exact executed source snapshots, raw observations, before/after identities and the run receipt. All performance, universal security and full feature parity gates remain false. Production edits and historical evidence rebinding remain outside this review.
